From aafa804fcc510d813e33d5fa5fc3118081e931db Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: exeroom536 <352890649+exeroom536@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 01:35:16 +0500
Subject: [PATCH 30/33] super-amd: follow the AMD firmware and driver in what
 they check

What mdadm writes is only of use if the AMD BIOS and the Windows driver
take it the same way:

- disks only behind controllers the AMD firmware uses for RAID (EFI
  variable AMD_RAID): a disk it does not see is missing from the volume
  there and in Windows.  --force takes it anyway (another machine, the
  disks on another controller to get at the data);
- no more arrays than the BIOS menu allows (12, a Legacy disk counted),
  and no volume of more disks than the menu and rcadm create (8), unless
  --force: the BIOS and Windows took volumes of up to 12 disks;
- Legacy (pass-through) records the BIOS menu broke - the disk is no
  longer offered for boot - are repaired, each Legacy disk its own;
- a disk whose LBA 0 changed under AMD's partition (boot code, a disk
  signature) gets the new checksum in its label: without it the BIOS
  and the Windows driver take it for a non-RAID disk;
- TRIM is off where the AMD driver does not allow it: on parity volumes,
  and on mirrors whose members do not read zeroes after TRIM.

Written by Claude Opus 5.5 and Claude Fable 5.2, AI models by Anthropic.
